      "writtenOn": "2017-07-27T00:51:54Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027ve now redone it to using a channel to implement the maximum number of concurrent goroutines. I\u0027ve done it slightly differently than context.go because the thing generating the blocks to process isn\u0027t the same thing that processes the blocks, and it seemed to me to be more confusing to have one select handling all of the different pieces.\n\nI think that the the implementation that I\u0027ll be uploading as my next patchset (which is essentially MapReduce) is markedly less readable than the previous patchset, and that the main redeeming advantage is that it has slightly more parallelism (although I don\u0027t detect faster performance) because it allows reading data from the file in parallel with aggregating the results of processing a block.\n\nAnother option would be to just keep this \"for\" loop but to extract the channel read (\"threadSucceeded :\u003d statusChannel\") into the main body of the \"for\" loop, if the concern is merely to make it clear that the \"for\" loop won\u0027t busy-wait.",
